00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:03.620
You've already learned about the basic structure of an RNN.

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:08.620
In this video, you see how backpropagation in a recurrent neural network works.

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:12.020
As usual, when you implement this in one of the programming frameworks,

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:16.620
often the programming framework will automatically take care of backpropagation.

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:22.220
But I think it's still useful to have a rough sense of how backprop works in RNNs.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:23.620
Let's take a look.

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:28.420
You've seen how for a proper prop, you would use, compute these

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:33.820
activations from left to right as follows in the neural network,

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:37.620
until you've output all of the predictions.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:44.020
In backprop, as you might already have guessed, you end up carrying backpropagation

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:48.720
calculations in basically the opposite direction of the forward prop arrows.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.820
So let's go through the forward propagation calculation.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:01:02.620
You're given this input sequence x1, x2, x3 up to x, t, x.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:12.220
And then using x1 and say a0, you're going to compute the activation at time step one.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:21.920
And then together, x2, together with a1, I use to compute a2 and then a3 and so on,

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:25.720
up to a, t, x.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:29.920
And then to actually compute a1, you also need the parameters,

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.620
I'll just draw this in green, w, a, and b, a.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:35.620
Those are the parameters that I use to compute a1.

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:39.520
And then these parameters are actually used for every single time step.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:47.320
So these parameters are actually used to compute a2, a3, and so on.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:50.820
All the activations up to last time step depend on the parameters,

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:52.820
w, a, and b, a.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:55.220
Let's keep fleshing out this graph.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:02:00.920
Now given a1, your neural network can then compute the first prediction,

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:10.520
y hat 1, and then the second time step, y hat 2, y hat 3, and so on,

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:15.620
with y hat t, y here.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:18.620
And let me again draw the parameters of a different color.

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:27.620
So to compute y hat, you need the parameters w, y, as well as b, y.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:32.020
And this goes into this node, as well as all the others.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:34.220
These are just folders in green as well.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:39.020
Next, in order to compute back propagation, you need a loss function.

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:45.620
So let's define an element y's loss first, which is, suppose that for a certain word in the sequence,

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:51.020
it is a person's name, so maybe y t is 1, and your neural network outputs some probability,

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:55.020
maybe 0.1 of that particular word being a person's name.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:03:00.420
So I'm going to define this as the standard logistic regression loss,

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:04.020
also called the cross entropy loss.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:10.220
This was a lot familiar to you from when we were previously looking at binary classification problems.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:14.520
So this is the loss associated with a single prediction at a single position,

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:18.220
or the single time step t for a single word.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:21.720
Let's now define the overall loss of the entire sequence.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:30.020
So L will be defined as the sum of overall t equals 1 to, I guess, t x or t y.

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:39.020
t x is equal to t y in this example of the losses for the individual time steps, comma, y, t.

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:47.520
And then, so this capital L, without this superscript t, this is the loss for the entire sequence.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:56.320
So in our computation graph, to compute the loss given y hat 1, you can then compute the loss for the first time step,

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:03.220
given that you compute the loss for the second time step, the loss for the third time step,

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:08.120
and so on, the loss for the final time step.

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:15.320
And then lastly, to compute the overall loss, we will take these and sum them all up

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:23.220
to compute the final L using that equation, which is the sum of the individual per time step losses.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:26.020
So this is the computation graph.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:32.420
And from the earlier examples you've seen of backpropagation, it shouldn't surprise you that backprop

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:39.720
then just requires doing computations or passing messages in the opposite directions

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:43.620
to all of the forward propagation steps, arrows.

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:46.120
And so you end up doing that.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:51.620
And that then allows you to compute all the appropriate quantities,

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:55.620
then lets you then take derivatives with respect to the parameters

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:58.720
and update the parameters using gradient descent.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:05.220
Now in this backpropagation procedure, the most significant messages to be passed,

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:11.420
the most significant recursive calculation is this one, which goes from the right to the left.

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:17.420
And that's what gives this algorithm as well a pretty fanciful name called backpropagation through time.

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:22.320
And the motivation for this name is that for forward prop, you are scanning from left to right

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:27.720
in increasing indices of the time t, whereas for backpropagation, you're going from right to left,

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:29.420
you're kind of going backwards in time.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:33.820
So this gives this, I think, really cool name backpropagation through time,

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:35.820
where you're going backwards in time.

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:40.120
That phrase really makes it sound like you need a time machine to implement this algorithm,

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:45.520
but I just thought that backprop through time was just one of the coolest names for an algorithm.

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:51.020
So I hope that gives you a sense of how forward prop and backprop in an RNN works.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:55.320
Now so far, you've only seen this main motivating example of an RNN

00:05:55.320 --> 00:06:00.420
in which the length of the input sequence was equal to the length of the output sequence.

